  pages={1-10},
  abstract={Personalised medicine is becoming a central feature of modern healthcare, aiming to improve the accuracy of diagnostics through consideration of the patient’s individualised factors. Traditional artificial intelligence (AI) models typically operate based on single-modality input, while most of the decisions made in a real-world setting need heterogeneous data from sources such as biological tests, radiological images, and clinical history. This requires multimodal deep learning (MDL) frameworks that can learn, extract, and amalgamate the complementary information across modalities. Despite recent advances, challenges persist in modality alignment, handling missing data, and ensuring clinically meaningful interpretability. In response, this paper offers a structured overview of recent MDL approaches for medical diagnosis, proposing a taxonomy that distinguishes traditional strategies from advanced mechanisms. Representative works are compared in terms of contributions, limitations, and perspectives.},
