  pages={1-6},
  abstract={The issue of lung cancer continues to be a major problem in the world, and this prompts a need to ensure proper diagnostic interventions are established at an early stage. The presented investigation provides a comparative study of classical machine-learning algorithms and modern deep-learning architectures for classifying lung histopathological images. Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression, and Support Vector Machines showed test values of 83.2, 77.3, and 60.6, respectively. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Deep Belief Networks (DBNs) based on deep learning significantly improved performance, achieving test accuracies of 93.9% and 86.0%, respectively. Based on these results, a new Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) model was proposed to estimate sequential dependencies among spatially partitioned areas of medical imagery. By combining time-distributed convolutional feature encoding with bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) temporal modeling, the proposed architecture can identify complex hierarchical structures in histopathological data. The empirical findings show that the RNN-based model achieved training accuracy of 99.5% and test accuracy of 98.3%, indicating very little generalization loss.},
  keywords={Modeling;Accuracy;Lungs;Machine learning;Machining;Convolutional neural networks;Printing;Recurrent neural networks;Support vector machines;Lung cancer;Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN);Deep Belief Networks (DBN);Histopathology;Image processing;Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN)},
