  title={Non-Invasive Brain Tumor Diagnosis Through EEG Signal Analysis Using Convolutional Neural Networks}, 
  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={715-720},
  abstract={Brain tumors can be life threatening if not detected in a timely manner. They usually lead to severe neurological decline and shortened lifespan. While brain tumors can be detected through MRI and CT scans, those procedures are costly, time-consuming, and in some cases, not readily available, particularly in developing countries. On the other hand, Electroencephalography (EEG) brain activity scan is relatively cheaper, quicker, and most importantly, it is non-invasive which makes it not only safe for the patient but also easier and more ethical to perform in children. Certain patterns of brain activity associated with tumors can be detected using EEG scans.This work focuses on developing automated brain tumor detection algorithms based on deep learning techniques using EEG data. This system is designed to work with a lightweight Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) which will perform identification of time and multi spatial EEG recording patterns, which eliminates the need for manual feature construction. The developed system, after training on public datasets, demonstrates more than adequate accuracy, sensitivity and specificity in detecting tumor and non-tumor EEG which allows for its practical use in a clinical setting. The system has been designed to work in real time on portable EEG machines which can be efficiently used in developing countries where healthcare systems experience MRI or CT scan bottlenecks.},
  keywords={Deep learning;Hands;Magnetic resonance imaging;Brain tumors;Medical services;Developing countries;Electroencephalography;Real-time systems;Convolutional neural networks;Artificial intelligence;ECG;Brain Tumor Detection;Deep Learning;Convolutional Neural Network;Biomedical Signal Processing},
