  abstract={Since the prognosis and survival outcomes are better when cancer is detected early, especially at an early stage, and since many of these diagnostics have low sensitivity and specificity, Destroytal detection becomes more suitable for high-risk groups. In this work, we present a systematic study that integrates multiple omics data types: cfDNA fragmentomics, DNA methylation signatures, ctDNA variants, proteomic biomarkers (in progress), together with clinical meta data, for reliable early cancer detection in a comprehensive multi-omics integration framework. We assess several types of integration methods, such as early fusion, late decision-level stacking, and graph-based models. The ensemble stacking model of Random Forest, XGBoost and Deep Learning outperformed the early fusion methods as well as graph convolutional networks (GCN), which still yielded strong performances (AUC 0.958) >> AUC:0.961, Sensitivity :0.902, Specificity : 0.918 SHAP and GCN attention scores also identified biologically interpret able features, such as loss of cfDNA end-motif "CCG" (Lung), hypomethylation at chr5:150–200kb (CRC), co-elevation of AFP & DCP (HCC) and age > 60 with high-risk occupational exposure. These findings highlight the potential of integrative, multi-modal learning to advance cancer diagnosis and shed light on new directions for clinical translation and biomarker identification.},
