  pages={1-6},
  abstract={Lung cancer is still one of the most fatal cancers worldwide, mainly because it is usually detected at a late stage. Early and accurate diagnosis can significantly improve a patient’s chances of survival. With the growth of artificial intelligence, machine learning models have become valuable tools for analysing medical images such as CT scans and X-rays. This work presents an ensemble-based system that combines several machine learning classifiers, including CNN, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting, to improve prediction accuracy. The system uses CNN for deep feature extraction and traditional classifiers for refined decision-making. Experimental results show that the ensemble model performs better than individual models in terms of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Overall, the proposed framework demonstrates how ensemble learning can support reliable early detection of lung cancer and help clinicians make better decisions.},
