  title={Deep Learning Based Biomarker Discovery and Cancer Subtype Classification Using Cross-Domain Multi-Omics Integration}, 
  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={637-644},
  abstract={Cancer remains one of the most lethal diseases globally, causing millions of deaths annually. Biomarkers play a critical role in the progression of cancer. Their identification aids clinicians in providing targeted and prioritized treatment to patients. For effective cancer and biomarker detection, the utilization of omics data is essential. In this study, a cross-domain integration framework employing deep learning techniques, termed MBAE-Attn-DAGNN, is proposed for biomarker identification and breast cancer subtype classification. Initially, omics datasets comprising CNV, mRNA, and RPPA for breast cancer patients are utilized and processed through a Multi-Branch AutoEncoder (MBAE) to extract 100 representative features. For cross-domain integration, Attention (Attn)-based similarity is computed to perform network fusion. Subsequently, the extracted features and the fused data are input into a Deep Adaptive Graph Neural Network (DAGNN) for breast cancer subtype classification. The extracted features are further subjected to DAVID analysis, which identifies the top two features from each omics type. The classification performance of the proposed MBAE-Attn-DAGNN model is assessed using multiple evaluation metrics, demonstrating an accuracy of 89%.},
  keywords={Deep learning;Measurement;Adaptive systems;Communication systems;Biomarkers;Feature extraction;Breast cancer;mRNA;Graph neural networks;Data mining;Cross-Domain Integration;Multi-Branch;Attention Networks;Deep Adaptive;Deep Learning},
