  booktitle={2026 IEEE 5th International Maghreb Meeting of the Conference on Sciences and Techniques of Automatic Control and Computer Engineering (MI-STA)}, 
  title={Advancing Cancer Surveillance in Libya: Insights from the Largest National Dataset Using Geospatial and Cluster Analysis}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={180-187},
  abstract={Cancer remains a major public health concern in Libya, where limited digital surveillance systems hinder strategic planning and early detection efforts. Using 27,369 validated patient records from the Libyan National Cancer Registry, this study provides a nationwide analysis of cancer incidence, geographic variation, and demographic disparities. After rigorous preprocessing, statistical and geospatial analyses revealed a strong concentration of cases along the northern coastal corridor, led by Zawia, Tripoli, Sabratha and Surman forming the primary costal cluster, while cities such as Sabha, and Gharian forming the primary inland cluster. Breast Cancer was the most common malignancy nationwide and appeared more than twice as frequently in coastal regions. Sex-stratified assessments showed marked female predominance in 19 of the 20 most affected cities, with the largest gap observed in Surman ($\approx 1.77: 1$ female-to-male ratio). K-Prototypes clustering identified four patient phenotypes: (1) a younger coastal Breast Cancer cluster, (2) an older inland cluster dominated by Colorectal and Lung Cancers, (3) a pediatric group enriched for hematologic malignancies, and (4) a heterogeneous subgroup with incomplete diagnostic data. By integrating descriptive analytics, geospatial mapping, and machine learning, this study offers a comprehensive yet compact characterization of cancer distribution in Libya and provides actionable insights for region-specific screening, resource allocation, and national cancer surveillance planning.},
